{#
  The ignore file BuildKit applies to the scenario Dockerfile in place of the repository root one.

  An image built from the repository gets the root file verbatim plus a re-include block, rather than a hand-picked
  subset, so a rule added to `.gitignore` reaches this build too. Docker's matcher is last-match-wins and, unlike git's,
  still descends into an excluded directory to honour a later negation, so the trailing block is enough to bring back
  the built artifacts while every other exclusion keeps applying.

  An image built from its own deployment directory copies nothing out of its context, so everything is excluded and
  the build is sent nothing at all -- the directory's `secrets.env` included.
#}
# AUTO-GENERATED by `et-cli` for the `{{ name }}` scenario -- do not edit.
# Regenerate with `mise run regen-verification`.
#
{% if repository %}
# BuildKit reads this file instead of the repository root `.dockerignore` when building the
# Dockerfile beside it, so the root file keeps governing the CI images built from `./Dockerfile`
# and only this build sees the re-includes at the end.

{{ root_dockerignore }}
# Re-admit the built module artifacts this scenario serves.
# The rules above drop every `*.wasm` and `*.onnx`, which is what keeps the CI images small; these
# negations put back the `pkg/` directories this image copies, and nothing else.
{%     for built in built_artifacts %}
!{{ built }}
{%     endfor %}
{% else %}
# The Dockerfile beside this file copies nothing from its build context, so the context is empty.
*
{% endif %}
